  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Showers and perhaps a brief thunderstorm are \npossible over the southern tier of PA late this afternoon/\nevening and perhaps a few more showers late tonight. Dry \nconditions for Friday before unsettled weather returns for the \nweekend.\n\nA cold front nearly bisecting PA from NE to SW will push slowly\nsouth through the rest of the state this afternoon and evening \nbefore stalling out just south of the Mason/Dixon line tonight \nand Friday.\n\nA few weak waves of low pressure riding east along this boundary\nwell bring periods of showers to Southern PA, and perhaps even a\nbrief thunderstorm late this afternoon or early this evening\nacross the Laurel Highlands. Only isolated showers are expected\nnorth of US Route 22. Rainfall will vary greatly from near zero\nin some spots to as much as 0.25+ inch where a brief\nthunderstorm occurs given MU CAPE climbing to between 750-1000\nJ/KG for a few hours late this afternoon.  \n\nLarger scale, more subtle forcing beneath the right entrance \nregion of a 130 kt upper jet stretched from Lake Huron to the \nMiddle St Lawrence River Valley will help to produce a few light\nshowers across the NW and Ncent Mtns of PA late today and early\nevening, but QPF will be limited to a few or svrl hundredths of\nan inch in most spots up north.\n\nThe aforementioned surface cold front won't likely push through\nthe southern third of the CWA until this evening, so there will\nbe additional SHRA today with up to 60 pct in Somerset Co. A \nstray shower (no thunder) could also affect Warren/McKean Cos as\nthe cool air aloft helps create some very weak instability \nalong the NY border and the cold front passes. However, \ninstability over the CWA will be low (<500J/kg in the far SW), \nso only some isolated thunder is expected - and only in the \nLaurels. The risk for heavy rain has decreased markedly through \nthe last 48 hrs of model runs, and the WPC ERO MRGL risk area \nwon't touch PA anymore (stays to our S) with the new guidance to\nbe issued soon. The most-possible time for rain will be the \nafternoon and early evening as some of the weak convection moves\nin from the SW. The dewpoints don't come down until the front \narrives (mid- late morning N, close to sunset in the S). So, it \nmay still feel a little muggy today, esp from I-80 southward \nwith 65-70F Tds all day long. \n\nHigh pressure builds behind the cold front, giving way to a dry\nand mainly sunny Friday. AM valley fog is almost a given. The\nextent of the fog is a question, but we'll paint it in all the\nnormal nrn valleys at this point. \n\nAs SFC high pressure moves well off the Mid Atlantic and \nSouthern New England Coast, low level moisture lurking just \nsouth of the Mason/Dixon line Fri night will push steadily NE \nacross the state on Saturday within broad llvl flow from the \nsouth to southwest. A notable axis of streamline convergence \nshifting gradually east across Central PA late Sat afternoon/Sat\nnight will provide increasing llvl theta-E advection and \nnumerous showers with a few to several tenths of an inch of \nrainfall likely across at least the SE half to two thirds of \nthe CWA by early Sunday. A few PM TSRA possible Saturday and/or \nSunday afternoons, especially over the western mtns. A more- \nactive (wetter) pattern is in store for Mon-Tues. But, the flat \nupper ridge over the southern half of the CONUS will make it \ndifficult for deep moisture to make it into PA ahead of any \nshots of atmos forcing. The week could end dry as the upper \nridge pumps up over the central CONUS.",
